-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 1: BACnet Priority Array: Understanding and Resolving
--            Command Conflicts
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'reference'),
  'BACnet Priority Array: Understanding and Resolving Command Conflicts',
  'bacnet-priority-array-command-conflicts',
  'Reference guide to the BACnet 16-level priority array as defined in ASHRAE 135, covering priority level assignments, Relinquish Default behavior, and systematic troubleshooting of command conflicts.',
  E'# BACnet Priority Array: Understanding and Resolving Command Conflicts\n\nThe priority array is the mechanism BACnet uses to determine which command source controls a Commandable object (such as an Analog Output, Binary Output, or Multi-State Output). Misunderstanding how priority levels interact is the most common cause of \"the BAS won''t let me control this point\" calls.\n\n## How the Priority Array Works\n\nEvery Commandable BACnet object contains a Priority Array property with 16 slots, numbered 1 (highest) through 16 (lowest). Each slot can hold a command value or NULL. The object''s Present Value is determined by the highest-priority (lowest-numbered) non-NULL entry in the array.\n\nWhen all 16 slots are NULL, the object uses its Relinquish Default value.\n\n## Priority Level Assignments\n\nASHRAE Standard 135 defines names for specific priority levels. The remaining levels are available for application use.\n\n| Priority | Name (per ASHRAE 135) | Typical Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Manual-Life Safety | Fire, smoke control, stairwell pressurization |\n| 2 | Automatic-Life Safety | Automatic safety shutdowns |\n| 3 | (Available) | Rarely used |\n| 4 | (Available) | Rarely used |\n| 5 | Critical Equipment Control | Equipment protection interlocks |\n| 6 | Minimum On/Off | Minimum on/off timers |\n| 7 | (Available) | Sometimes used for demand limiting |\n| 8 | Manual Operator | Front-end operator overrides |\n| 9 | (Available) | Application-specific |\n| 10 | (Available) | Application-specific |\n| 11 | (Available) | Application-specific |\n| 12 | (Available) | Application-specific |\n| 13 | (Available) | Application-specific |\n| 14 | (Available) | Commonly used for schedule writes |\n| 15 | (Available) | Commonly used for control sequences |\n| 16 | (Available) | Commonly used as default/fallback |\n\nPriority levels 1, 2, 5, 6, and 8 are named in the standard. The naming of levels 3, 4, 7, and 9 through 16 as \"Available\" means they are defined by the application or project, not by the standard itself.\n\n## Relinquish Default\n\nThe Relinquish Default is a separate property on every Commandable object. It specifies what Present Value the object should assume when all 16 priority array slots are NULL. For a Binary Output, this is typically Inactive (off). For an Analog Output, it might be 0% or a safe fallback position.\n\n## Reading the Priority Array\n\nMost BAS front-ends provide a way to view the full priority array for a Commandable object. The information you need:\n\n```\nFor each of the 16 slots:\n  Slot number | Value or NULL | Which application wrote it\n\nExample - Analog Output (Cooling Valve):\n  Priority 1:  NULL\n  Priority 2:  NULL\n  Priority 3:  NULL\n  Priority 4:  NULL\n  Priority 5:  0%        ← Freeze protect interlock (closed)\n  Priority 6:  NULL\n  Priority 7:  NULL\n  Priority 8:  75%       ← Operator override\n  Priority 9:  NULL\n  Priority 10: NULL\n  Priority 11: NULL\n  Priority 12: NULL\n  Priority 13: NULL\n  Priority 14: NULL\n  Priority 15: 60%       ← Control loop output\n  Priority 16: NULL\n  Relinquish Default: 0%\n\n  Present Value = 0% (Priority 5 wins)\n```\n\nIn this example, the freeze protect interlock at priority 5 overrides both the operator override at priority 8 and the control loop at priority 15.\n\n## Common Command Conflict Scenarios\n\n### Scenario 1: Operator Override Stuck\n\n```\nSymptom: Equipment does not respond to automatic control.\nCause: An operator wrote a value at priority 8 and never released it.\nFix: Write NULL to priority 8 (relinquish the override).\n```\n\nThis is the single most common priority array issue. An operator commands a value during troubleshooting and forgets to release it. The automatic control sequence, typically running at priority 14 or 15, cannot override the priority 8 command.\n\n### Scenario 2: Schedule Conflicts with Control\n\n```\nSymptom: Equipment snaps to a fixed value at schedule transitions.\nCause: Schedule writes at priority 14, control loop writes at priority 15.\nFix: Verify that the schedule writes to the correct object and uses\n     the intended priority level. If both schedule and control need\n     to coexist, the schedule should command a setpoint or mode, not\n     the output directly.\n```\n\n### Scenario 3: Safety Interlock Prevents All Control\n\n```\nSymptom: Output is locked at 0% or 100% regardless of demand.\nCause: A life safety or equipment protection command at priority 1-6\n       is active.\nFix: Investigate and resolve the safety condition. Do not attempt\n     to override a life safety priority from a lower priority level.\n```\n\n### Scenario 4: Unknown Command Source\n\n```\nSymptom: Present Value does not match any known control sequence.\nCause: A third-party integration, schedule, or test tool wrote a\n       value at a priority level and was not cleaned up.\nFix: Read the full priority array to identify which level holds\n     the unexpected value, then trace which application wrote it.\n```\n\n## Troubleshooting Procedure\n\n1. Read the full 16-level priority array for the affected object.\n2. Identify every non-NULL entry and its priority level.\n3. The lowest-numbered non-NULL entry determines the Present Value.\n4. For each non-NULL entry, determine which application or operator placed it.\n5. If the command is no longer needed, write NULL to that priority level to relinquish it.\n6. Verify the Present Value updates correctly after relinquishing.\n\n## Writing and Relinquishing Commands\n\n### Writing a Command\n\nA BACnet WriteProperty or WritePropertyMultiple request targets a specific priority level. The command includes:\n\n- Object identifier (e.g., Analog Output 1)\n- Property: Present Value\n- Value: the desired output\n- Priority: the level to write at (1-16)\n\n### Relinquishing a Command\n\nTo release a priority level, write NULL to that slot. This removes the command and allows lower-priority commands or the Relinquish Default to take effect.\n\n```\nRelinquish example:\n  Object: Analog Output 1\n  Property: Present Value\n  Value: NULL\n  Priority: 8\n\n  Result: Priority 8 slot becomes NULL.\n  Present Value reverts to the next non-NULL priority,\n  or Relinquish Default if all slots are NULL.\n```\n\n## Best Practices for Priority Assignment\n\n- Document which application owns which priority level before commissioning.\n- Use priority 8 for temporary operator overrides and always relinquish when done.\n- Use priorities 14-16 for normal automatic control sequences.\n- Reserve priorities 1-2 for actual life safety functions only.\n- Avoid writing to priority 1 from non-safety applications.\n- Include priority array status on operator graphics so technicians can see active commands at a glance.\n- Implement override timers that automatically relinquish priority 8 after a configurable duration.\n\n## Platform-Specific Notes\n\nDifferent BAS platforms may use different default priority levels for their control programs, schedules, and operator commands. Always verify the priority assignments in the specific platform documentation for the version you are using.\n\n## References\n\n- [ASHRAE Standard 135-2020 - BACnet: A Data Communication Protocol for Building Automation and Control Networks](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/bacnet)\n- [BACnet International - BACnet Overview](https://bacnet.org/bacnet-overview/)\n- [ASHRAE Standing Standard Project Committee 135 (SSPC 135)](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/standards-and-guidelines/standards-addenda/addenda-to-standard-135-2020)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

