-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 2: Modbus RTU and TCP Register Mapping for BAS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'reference'),
  'Modbus RTU and TCP Register Mapping for BAS Integrations',
  'modbus-rtu-tcp-register-mapping-bas',
  'Reference guide to Modbus RTU and TCP integration in building automation, covering register types, function codes, addressing conventions, byte order, and common field troubleshooting based on the Modbus Application Protocol specification.',
  E'# Modbus RTU and TCP Register Mapping for BAS Integrations\n\nModbus is the second most common protocol encountered in building automation after BACnet. It appears in energy meters, variable frequency drives, generators, boilers, lighting panels, and many third-party devices. Unlike BACnet, Modbus provides no self-description—you must have the device''s register map documentation before you can integrate it.\n\n## Modbus Variants\n\nThe Modbus Application Protocol defines the data model and function codes. The transport layer determines how messages are framed and delivered.\n\n| Variant | Transport | Framing | Error Check | Typical Use |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Modbus RTU | Serial (RS-485, RS-232) | Binary with timing gaps | CRC-16 | Field devices, meters, VFDs |\n| Modbus ASCII | Serial (RS-485, RS-232) | ASCII hex with start/end markers | LRC | Rarely used in BAS |\n| Modbus TCP | Ethernet (TCP/IP) | MBAP header, no CRC | TCP reliability | Ethernet-capable devices, gateways |\n\nModbus RTU over RS-485 is the most common variant in BAS field devices. Modbus TCP is used when the device has an Ethernet port or when a serial-to-TCP gateway is deployed.\n\n## Register Types\n\nThe Modbus data model defines four register types, as specified in the Modbus Application Protocol Specification V1.1b3:\n\n| Type | Access | Size | Address Range (Documentation Convention) | Function Codes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Coils | Read/Write | 1 bit | 00001-09999 | 01 (read), 05 (write single), 15 (write multiple) |\n| Discrete Inputs | Read Only | 1 bit | 10001-19999 | 02 (read) |\n| Input Registers | Read Only | 16 bits | 30001-39999 | 04 (read) |\n| Holding Registers | Read/Write | 16 bits | 40001-49999 | 03 (read), 06 (write single), 16 (write multiple) |\n\nThe address ranges shown (1xxxx, 3xxxx, 4xxxx) are a documentation convention. On the wire, all register addresses are 0-based. A register documented as 40001 is addressed as register 0 in the actual Modbus frame.\n\n## Function Codes\n\nThe most commonly used function codes in BAS integrations:\n\n| Code | Name | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| 01 | Read Coils | Read one or more coil (bit) values |\n| 02 | Read Discrete Inputs | Read one or more discrete input bits |\n| 03 | Read Holding Registers | Read one or more 16-bit holding registers |\n| 04 | Read Input Registers | Read one or more 16-bit input registers |\n| 05 | Write Single Coil | Write a single coil (ON/OFF) |\n| 06 | Write Single Register | Write a single 16-bit holding register |\n| 15 | Write Multiple Coils | Write multiple coils in one request |\n| 16 | Write Multiple Registers | Write multiple holding registers in one request |\n\n## Addressing: The Off-by-One Problem\n\nThe most common Modbus integration error is an off-by-one addressing mistake:\n\n```\nDevice documentation says: Register 40001\nModbus wire address:       Register 0 (0x0000)\n\nDevice documentation says: Register 40002\nModbus wire address:       Register 1 (0x0001)\n```\n\nSome BAS platforms add 1 automatically (expecting the documentation convention). Others expect the raw wire address. Always test by reading a register with a known value to confirm the addressing mode your BAS platform uses.\n\n## Data Types and Byte Order\n\nModbus registers are 16 bits wide. Values larger than 16 bits span multiple consecutive registers.\n\n| Data Type | Registers | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| 16-bit integer (INT16, UINT16) | 1 | Single register, big-endian per Modbus standard |\n| 32-bit integer (INT32, UINT32) | 2 | Two consecutive registers |\n| 32-bit float (IEEE 754) | 2 | Two consecutive registers |\n| 64-bit value | 4 | Four consecutive registers |\n\n### Byte Order Variations\n\nThe Modbus standard specifies big-endian byte order within a single 16-bit register (most significant byte first). However, when a value spans two registers, the register order varies by device manufacturer:\n\n| Byte Order | Register Order | Also Called |\n|---|---|---|\n| Big-endian | High word first (AB CD) | Modbus standard |\n| Little-endian | Low word first (CD AB) | Reversed |\n| Mid-big-endian | BA DC | Byte-swapped |\n| Mid-little-endian | DC BA | Byte-swapped reversed |\n\nThe device documentation should specify the byte order. If it does not, test with a known value. A 32-bit float of 100.0 (IEEE 754: 0x42C80000) should read as:\n\n```\nBig-endian:        Register N = 0x42C8, Register N+1 = 0x0000\nLittle-endian:     Register N = 0x0000, Register N+1 = 0x42C8\nMid-big-endian:    Register N = 0xC842, Register N+1 = 0x0000\nMid-little-endian: Register N = 0x0000, Register N+1 = 0xC842\n```\n\n## RS-485 Wiring for Modbus RTU\n\nModbus RTU uses RS-485 serial communication:\n\n| Parameter | Typical Setting |\n|---|---|\n| Baud rate | 9600 or 19200 (must match all devices) |\n| Data bits | 8 |\n| Parity | None (most common in BAS) or Even |\n| Stop bits | 1 (if parity) or 2 (if no parity, per Modbus standard) |\n| Wiring | Shielded twisted pair, daisy chain topology |\n| Termination | 120 ohm resistor at each end of the bus |\n| Max devices | 32 per segment (without repeater) |\n| Max cable length | 1200 m (4000 ft) at 9600 baud |\n\nAll devices on the same RS-485 bus must use identical serial parameters (baud rate, parity, stop bits).\n\n## Modbus TCP Specifics\n\nModbus TCP uses standard Ethernet and TCP/IP. Key differences from RTU:\n\n| Aspect | Modbus RTU | Modbus TCP |\n|---|---|---|\n| Default port | N/A (serial) | TCP 502 |\n| Unit ID | Slave address (1-247) | Unit ID in MBAP header (often 1 or 255) |\n| Error checking | CRC-16 | TCP reliability |\n| Connection | Shared bus | Point-to-point TCP socket |\n| Max concurrent | 1 master per bus | Multiple clients per server |\n\n## Polling Strategy\n\nModbus is a request/response protocol—the master (BAS controller) polls each device. Polling too aggressively can overload devices or saturate the serial bus.\n\n| Guideline | Recommendation |\n|---|---|\n| Minimum poll interval (RTU) | 1-2 seconds per device |\n| Group reads | Read contiguous registers in a single request |\n| Max registers per read | 125 holding registers per request (protocol limit) |\n| Timeout | 1-3 seconds for RTU, 3-5 seconds for TCP |\n| Retries | 1-2 retries before declaring device offline |\n| Stagger polls | Avoid polling all devices simultaneously |\n\n## Common Troubleshooting\n\n| Symptom | Likely Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| No response from device | Wrong slave address, baud rate, or wiring | Verify address and serial parameters; check wiring |\n| CRC errors | Noise, wrong parity/stop bits, or cable length | Check serial parameters, shielding, and termination |\n| Values read as zero | Wrong register address (off-by-one) | Adjust register offset |\n| Values read incorrectly | Wrong byte order or data type | Test byte order with a known value |\n| Intermittent timeouts | Bus overloaded or cable too long | Reduce poll rate or add a repeater |\n| Writes have no effect | Wrong function code or register is read-only | Confirm register type (holding vs input) |\n\n## Integration Checklist\n\n```\nBefore Starting:\n  [ ] Obtain the device register map from the manufacturer\n  [ ] Confirm Modbus variant (RTU, TCP, ASCII)\n  [ ] Confirm serial parameters or TCP port\n  [ ] Confirm device slave/unit ID address\n\nDuring Configuration:\n  [ ] Verify addressing convention (0-based vs 1-based)\n  [ ] Confirm data types and byte order for multi-register values\n  [ ] Test a known register value before mapping all points\n  [ ] Set appropriate poll interval and timeout\n\nAfter Commissioning:\n  [ ] Verify all mapped values against independent measurement\n  [ ] Confirm write commands take effect and read back correctly\n  [ ] Document register map, serial parameters, and polling config\n  [ ] Test failover behavior when device goes offline\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [Modbus Organization - Modbus Application Protocol Specification V1.1b3](https://modbus.org/specs.php)\n- [Modbus Organization - Modbus/TCP Implementation Guide](https://modbus.org/specs.php)\n- [Modbus Organization - Technical Resources](https://modbus.org/tech.php)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

