-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 3: Variable Frequency Drive (VFD) Network Integration in BAS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'how-to'),
  'Variable Frequency Drive (VFD) Network Integration in BAS',
  'vfd-network-integration-bas',
  'Field guide to integrating variable frequency drives over BACnet and Modbus in building automation, covering command ownership, feedback mapping, fault handling, and safety considerations.',
  E'# Variable Frequency Drive (VFD) Network Integration in BAS\n\nVariable frequency drives control motor speed on fans, pumps, cooling towers, and compressors. Integrating VFDs into the BAS over a network connection (BACnet MS/TP, BACnet/IP, or Modbus RTU) replaces analog 0-10V speed references and hardwired status/fault contacts with richer data and tighter control. This guide covers the practical steps, required documentation, and common pitfalls.\n\n## Integration Methods\n\n| Method | Protocol | Typical Connection |\n|---|---|---|\n| BACnet MS/TP | BACnet over RS-485 | Daisy chain to BAS controller MS/TP trunk |\n| BACnet/IP | BACnet over Ethernet | Ethernet switch on BAS VLAN |\n| Modbus RTU | Modbus over RS-485 | Daisy chain to BAS controller Modbus port |\n| Analog/Discrete | 0-10V speed reference, relay contacts | Hardwired to BAS controller I/O |\n\nNetwork integration provides more data points (speed feedback, power, current, fault codes, runtime hours) than analog wiring, but requires more careful commissioning.\n\n## Required Documentation\n\nBefore configuring anything, obtain from the VFD manufacturer:\n\n```\nRequired Documents:\n  [ ] VFD communication manual (not just the general installation manual)\n  [ ] BACnet PICS (Protocol Implementation Conformance Statement) or\n      Modbus register map\n  [ ] Parameter list for enabling network communication\n  [ ] Default and configurable slave/device addresses\n  [ ] Supported baud rates and serial parameters (Modbus RTU)\n  [ ] Network fault behavior settings (what happens on communication loss)\n```\n\n## VFD Parameter Setup\n\nMost VFDs require specific parameters to be set before network communication works. Common parameters include:\n\n| Parameter Category | What to Configure |\n|---|---|\n| Communication source | Set speed reference source to network (not keypad or analog) |\n| Start/stop source | Set run command source to network (not terminal block) |\n| Protocol selection | Select BACnet or Modbus (if multi-protocol card) |\n| Address | Set unique device/slave address |\n| Baud rate | Match the BAS controller trunk speed |\n| Network fault action | Define behavior on communication loss (coast, stop, run at last speed, run at preset speed) |\n\nThe network fault action parameter is critical for safety. It determines what the VFD does if it loses communication with the BAS controller.\n\n## BACnet VFD Integration\n\nBACnet-capable VFDs typically expose these standard object types:\n\n| Object Type | Common Points |\n|---|---|\n| Analog Output | Speed reference (%) |\n| Analog Input | Output frequency (Hz), motor current (A), motor power (kW), DC bus voltage |\n| Binary Output | Run/stop command |\n| Binary Input | Running status, fault status, at-speed status, ready status |\n| Multi-State Input | Drive state (stopped, running, faulting, etc.) |\n\nThe speed reference is typically written as a percentage (0-100%) through a Commandable Analog Output object using the BACnet priority array.\n\n## Modbus VFD Integration\n\nModbus register maps vary by VFD manufacturer. Common register assignments:\n\n| Register (Typical) | Function | Data Type |\n|---|---|---|\n| Control word | Start, stop, direction, fault reset (bit-mapped) | UINT16 |\n| Speed reference | Target speed as percentage or frequency | UINT16 (scaled) |\n| Status word | Running, faulted, at-speed, ready (bit-mapped) | UINT16 |\n| Output frequency | Actual motor frequency | UINT16 (scaled, e.g., 0.01 Hz) |\n| Motor current | Actual motor current | UINT16 (scaled, e.g., 0.1 A) |\n| Motor power | Actual motor power | UINT16 (scaled) |\n| Fault code | Active fault identifier | UINT16 |\n\nScaling varies by manufacturer. A speed reference register might use 0-10000 to represent 0.00-100.00%, or 0-1000 for 0.0-100.0%. Always verify the scaling factor from the VFD communication manual.\n\n## Command Ownership and Safety\n\n### BACnet Priority Array\n\nWhen using BACnet, the speed reference and run command should be written at an appropriate priority level:\n\n- Safety interlocks (freeze protect, high static): Priority 5 (Critical Equipment Control)\n- Operator override: Priority 8 (Manual Operator)\n- Normal control sequence: Priority 14 or 15\n\n### Local/Remote and Hand-Off-Auto\n\nMost VFDs have a local/remote mode and may have a Hand-Off-Auto (HOA) switch:\n\n| Mode | Network Control | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Remote + Auto | Active | Normal operating mode |\n| Remote + Hand | Inactive | Manual local speed control |\n| Local | Inactive | Keypad control only |\n\nThe BAS should monitor the local/remote status and suppress alarms when the VFD is in local mode.\n\n### Fault Reset\n\nNetwork fault reset is typically a momentary command (pulse). The BAS should:\n\n1. Detect the fault status from the VFD.\n2. Present a \"Reset Fault\" button to the operator.\n3. Send a momentary reset command (write 1, wait, write 0).\n4. Limit automatic retries (e.g., 3 attempts with increasing delay) to prevent repeated restarts on a persistent mechanical fault.\n\n## Commissioning Checklist\n\n```\nPre-Commissioning:\n  [ ] VFD communication manual obtained\n  [ ] VFD parameters configured for network control\n  [ ] Network fault action set to safe state\n  [ ] Device address assigned and documented\n  [ ] RS-485 wiring verified (polarity, termination, daisy chain)\n      or Ethernet connection verified\n\nFunctional Tests:\n  [ ] BAS can read running status and speed feedback\n  [ ] BAS can start and stop the VFD\n  [ ] BAS can command speed from 0% to 100%\n  [ ] Speed feedback matches commanded speed within tolerance\n  [ ] Fault status is reported to BAS when VFD faults\n  [ ] Fault reset from BAS works correctly\n  [ ] HOA/local-remote status is visible in BAS\n  [ ] VFD goes to safe state on communication loss\n  [ ] VFD resumes network control when communication restores\n\nDocumentation:\n  [ ] Register/object map documented\n  [ ] Scaling factors documented\n  [ ] Network fault behavior documented\n  [ ] VFD parameters backed up\n```\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Prevention |\n|---|---|\n| VFD does not respond to network commands | Verify speed reference and run command sources are set to network in VFD parameters |\n| Speed reads 0 when motor is running | Check scaling factor; some VFDs report frequency in 0.01 Hz units |\n| Motor runs at full speed on communication loss | Set network fault action to stop or coast; never leave it at \"run at last speed\" for safety-critical applications |\n| Repeated fault resets cause mechanical damage | Limit automatic retry count and add delay between retries |\n| Multiple BAS points command the same VFD | Define a single command source; use BACnet priority array to manage conflicts |\n| Noise on RS-485 trunk from VFD | Route communication cables away from VFD power cables; use shielded twisted pair |\n\n## References\n\n- [ASHRAE - HVAC Systems and Equipment Handbook (Chapter on Variable Speed Drives)](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/ashrae-handbook)\n- [Modbus Organization - Modbus Application Protocol Specification](https://modbus.org/specs.php)\n- [ASHRAE Standard 135 - BACnet (Commandable Objects and Priority Array)](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/bacnet)\n- [NEMA MG 1 - Motors and Generators (Motor Application with Adjustable-Speed Drives)](https://www.nema.org/standards/view/nema-mg-1-motors-and-generators)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

