-- ARTICLE 4: BAS Alarm Rationalization and Management
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'best-practices'),
  'BAS Alarm Rationalization and Management',
  'bas-alarm-rationalization-management',
  'Best practices for BAS alarm management based on ISA-18.2 principles, covering alarm classification, priority assignment, nuisance alarm reduction, and routing strategies.',
  E'# BAS Alarm Rationalization and Management\n\nAlarm flooding is one of the most common operational failures in building automation. When operators receive hundreds of alarms per day, they stop responding to all of them—including the ones that matter. Alarm rationalization is the process of reviewing every alarm, classifying its purpose, assigning an appropriate priority, and removing or suppressing alarms that do not require operator action.\n\n## The Problem with Unmanaged Alarms\n\nA typical unrationalized BAS generates far more alarms than operators can process:\n\n| Metric | Unmanaged System | Target (per ISA-18.2 guidance) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Alarms per operator per day | 500-2000+ | Fewer than 144 (approximately 6 per hour) |\n| Percentage of nuisance alarms | 50-95% | Less than 10% |\n| Standing alarms (always active) | Dozens to hundreds | Zero |\n| Operator response to critical alarms | Inconsistent | Defined procedure for every alarm |\n\nThe ISA-18.2 standard (Management of Alarm Systems for the Process Industries) provides a framework for alarm management that applies directly to BAS operations.\n\n## Alarm Classification\n\nEvery alarm should be classified by its purpose. If an alarm does not require a specific operator response, it should not be an alarm.\n\n| Classification | Purpose | Operator Action Required | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Life Safety | Protect occupants | Immediate evacuation or safety response | Smoke detected, high CO levels |\n| Equipment Protection | Prevent equipment damage | Immediate investigation, possible shutdown | Freeze stat trip, high discharge pressure, VFD overcurrent |\n| Process/Comfort | Maintain building conditions | Timely investigation | Space temperature out of range, humidity high |\n| Maintenance | Schedule preventive action | Plan maintenance within defined window | Filter differential pressure high, runtime hours exceeded |\n| Informational | Status notification only | No action required | Equipment started, schedule changed |\n\nInformational notifications should not be alarms. They belong in event logs, not alarm queues.\n\n## Priority Assignment\n\nAlarm priority determines response urgency. A simple 4-level scheme works well for most BAS installations:\n\n| Priority | Response Time | Description | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Critical | Immediate (within minutes) | Life safety or imminent equipment damage | Smoke alarm, freeze stat trip |\n| High | Within 1 hour | Equipment at risk or significant comfort impact | Chiller fault, boiler lockout, server room temperature high |\n| Medium | Within 4 hours | Comfort deviation or efficiency loss | Zone temperature 5 degrees from setpoint, economizer failure |\n| Low | Within 24 hours | Maintenance advisory | Filter change needed, sensor drift detected |\n\n### Priority Assignment Questions\n\nFor each alarm point, answer:\n\n1. What could go wrong if the operator does not respond?\n2. How quickly must the operator respond to prevent that outcome?\n3. Can the operator actually do something about it from the BAS, or does it require a field visit?\n4. Is this alarm distinct from other alarms on the same equipment, or is it redundant?\n\n## Nuisance Alarm Reduction\n\nNuisance alarms are alarms that activate but do not indicate a real condition requiring operator action. Common causes and fixes:\n\n| Cause | Example | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Setpoint too tight | Space temp alarm at +/- 1 degree F | Widen deadband to +/- 2-3 degrees F |\n| No delay/debounce | Binary status chatters on/off | Add time delay (e.g., 5 minutes before alarming) |\n| Alarm during maintenance | Equipment alarms while being serviced | Implement maintenance bypass or shelving |\n| Alarm during unoccupied hours | Comfort alarms when building is empty | Suppress comfort alarms during unoccupied schedule |\n| Stale/broken sensor | Sensor reads out of range continuously | Fix or replace the sensor; do not just silence the alarm |\n| Duplicate alarms | Same condition generates alarms from multiple points | Consolidate to a single alarm per condition |\n| Standing alarms | Alarm is always active, never acknowledged or resolved | Investigate root cause; fix or remove the alarm |\n\n### Alarm Shelving\n\nAlarm shelving temporarily suppresses an alarm for a defined period (e.g., during maintenance). Requirements:\n\n- Shelving must have a maximum duration (e.g., 8 hours, 24 hours).\n- Shelved alarms must automatically unshelve after the timer expires.\n- An audit log must record who shelved the alarm and why.\n- Shelved alarms should be visible in a separate \"shelved\" list so they are not forgotten.\n\n## Alarm Routing\n\nNot every alarm goes to every operator. Route alarms based on:\n\n| Factor | Routing Decision |\n|---|---|\n| Building/zone | Route to the operator responsible for that building |\n| System type | Route HVAC alarms to HVAC operators, electrical to electrical |\n| Priority | Critical alarms go to on-call personnel via phone/text; low-priority to email or dashboard |\n| Time of day | After-hours alarms route to on-call; daytime to the control room |\n\n### Escalation\n\nDefine escalation paths for unacknowledged alarms:\n\n```\nEscalation Example:\n  0 min:  Alarm activates → primary operator (dashboard + notification)\n  15 min: Not acknowledged → secondary operator (text message)\n  30 min: Not acknowledged → supervisor (phone call)\n  60 min: Not acknowledged → facility manager (phone call)\n```\n\n## Rationalization Process\n\n### Step 1: Inventory\n\nExport the complete alarm point list from the BAS, including:\n\n- Point name and description\n- Equipment association\n- Current alarm limits and deadbands\n- Current priority\n- Alarm count for the past 90 days\n\n### Step 2: Classify and Prioritize\n\nFor each alarm, apply the classification and priority assignment described above. Remove or convert to events any alarms that do not require operator action.\n\n### Step 3: Set Appropriate Limits\n\nReview alarm setpoints, deadbands, and time delays. Tighten limits that are too loose (missing real conditions) and loosen limits that are too tight (generating nuisance alarms).\n\n### Step 4: Implement and Monitor\n\nApply the rationalized alarm configuration to the BAS. Monitor alarm rates for 30-60 days and adjust as needed.\n\n### Step 5: Ongoing Review\n\nAlarm rationalization is not a one-time project. Review alarm metrics quarterly:\n\n- Total alarm count per day\n- Top 10 most frequent alarms (these are rationalization candidates)\n- Standing alarms that have been active for more than 24 hours\n- Alarms with zero occurrences (may indicate broken sensors or unreachable setpoints)\n\n## Key Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Alarms per operator per 12-hour shift | Fewer than 72 (6 per hour) | ISA-18.2 |\n| Standing alarms | Zero | ISA-18.2 |\n| Time to first alarm response | Within the alarm priority response time | Site-specific |\n| Nuisance alarm percentage | Less than 10% of total | ISA-18.2 |\n| Alarms requiring no operator action | Zero (reclassify as events) | ISA-18.2 |\n\n## References\n\n- [ISA-18.2-2016 - Management of Alarm Systems for the Process Industries](https://www.isa.org/products/isa-18-2-2016-management-of-alarm-systems-for-the)\n- [ASHRAE Guideline 13-2015 - Specifying DDC Systems](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/ashrae-guideline-13-specifying-ddc-systems)\n- [NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3 - Guide to Operational Technology Security](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/82/r3/final)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

