-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 5: Demand Controlled Ventilation (DCV) with CO2 Sensors
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'best-practices'),
  'Demand Controlled Ventilation (DCV) with CO2 Sensors in BAS',
  'demand-controlled-ventilation-co2-sensors-bas',
  'Best practices for implementing CO2-based demand controlled ventilation in building automation, covering sensor selection, placement, control sequences, calibration, and references to ASHRAE 62.1 and Guideline 36.',
  E'# Demand Controlled Ventilation (DCV) with CO2 Sensors in BAS\n\nDemand controlled ventilation adjusts the amount of outdoor air supplied to a space based on real-time occupancy indicators, most commonly carbon dioxide (CO2) concentration. DCV reduces energy waste from over-ventilating unoccupied or partially occupied spaces while maintaining indoor air quality (IAQ) per ASHRAE Standard 62.1.\n\n## Why DCV Matters\n\nFixed outdoor air damper positions ventilate for maximum design occupancy at all times. In most buildings, actual occupancy varies significantly throughout the day.\n\n| Scenario | Fixed Ventilation | With DCV |\n|---|---|---|\n| Conference room (0 of 20 people) | Full outdoor air for 20 | Minimum outdoor air |\n| Conference room (20 of 20 people) | Full outdoor air for 20 | Full outdoor air for 20 |\n| Open office (30% occupied) | Full outdoor air for 100% | Outdoor air for 30% + area rate |\n| Auditorium (after event) | Full outdoor air for 500 | Ramps down as CO2 drops |\n\nDCV can reduce outdoor air heating and cooling energy by 20-60% in spaces with variable occupancy, according to ASHRAE research.\n\n## How CO2-Based DCV Works\n\nHumans exhale CO2 in proportion to their metabolic rate. By measuring indoor CO2 concentration and comparing it to outdoor CO2 levels, the BAS can estimate the ventilation rate per person and adjust outdoor air accordingly.\n\n### ASHRAE 62.1 Ventilation Rate Procedure\n\nASHRAE Standard 62.1 defines outdoor air requirements as the sum of two components:\n\n```\nOutdoor Air = (People Rate x Occupancy) + (Area Rate x Floor Area)\n\nWhere:\n  People Rate (Rp): cfm per person (varies by space type)\n  Area Rate (Ra):    cfm per sq ft (varies by space type)\n  Occupancy:         actual or estimated number of people\n```\n\nDCV addresses the people-dependent component (Rp x Occupancy). The area-dependent component (Ra x Floor Area) must always be provided regardless of occupancy.\n\n### CO2 as an Occupancy Proxy\n\nIndoor CO2 concentration above outdoor ambient indicates the presence of people. The relationship between CO2 levels and per-person ventilation rate is described in ASHRAE 62.1 Appendix C:\n\n| Indoor CO2 Above Outdoor (ppm) | Approximate Per-Person Ventilation |\n|---|---|\n| 300 ppm above outdoor | Approximately 25 cfm/person |\n| 500 ppm above outdoor | Approximately 15 cfm/person |\n| 700 ppm above outdoor | Approximately 10 cfm/person |\n\nOutdoor CO2 concentration varies by location and season, typically ranging from 400-450 ppm in most urban and suburban areas.\n\n## CO2 Sensor Selection\n\n| Parameter | Recommendation |\n|---|---|\n| Measurement range | 0-2000 ppm minimum |\n| Accuracy | +/- 50 ppm at 1000 ppm (per sensor manufacturer specification) |\n| Technology | Non-dispersive infrared (NDIR) is the standard for BAS applications |\n| Output | 0-10 V or 4-20 mA analog, or BACnet/Modbus digital |\n| Calibration | Automatic baseline correction (ABC) or manual calibration per manufacturer schedule |\n| Response time | Under 2 minutes for 90% step response |\n\n### Sensor Placement\n\nSensor placement directly affects control quality:\n\n| Guideline | Reason |\n|---|---|\n| Mount at 3-6 feet above floor (breathing zone) | CO2 stratification is minimal, but mounting near breathing height improves accuracy |\n| Avoid placement near doors, windows, and diffusers | Supply air and drafts dilute local CO2 readings |\n| Avoid placement near direct exhaust or return grilles | Return air CO2 may differ from zone CO2 |\n| Place in the occupied zone, not behind furniture or in corners | Dead air zones give delayed or unrepresentative readings |\n| One sensor per control zone (minimum) | Multiple zones with different occupancy patterns need separate sensors |\n\n### Return Air vs. Zone Sensors\n\nFor single-zone AHU systems, a return air CO2 sensor provides a reasonable average reading for the served space. For multi-zone systems (VAV), individual zone sensors provide better control because each zone may have different occupancy.\n\n## Control Sequences\n\n### Single-Zone AHU\n\nFor a single-zone AHU serving one space:\n\n```\nDCV Sequence:\n  1. Measure indoor CO2 (return air sensor or zone sensor)\n  2. Calculate CO2 setpoint based on design ventilation rate and\n     outdoor CO2 (typically 800-1000 ppm for office spaces)\n  3. Modulate outdoor air damper to maintain CO2 at or below setpoint\n  4. Maintain minimum outdoor air position for the area ventilation\n     rate (Ra component) regardless of CO2 reading\n  5. Override to maximum outdoor air during economizer operation\n```\n\n### Multi-Zone VAV System\n\nASHRAE Guideline 36 provides detailed DCV sequences for VAV systems. The key concepts:\n\n```\nGuideline 36 DCV (simplified):\n  1. Each zone has a CO2 sensor and a minimum outdoor air setpoint\n  2. Zone minimum airflow is the greater of:\n     - Ventilation minimum (area rate + occupancy-based rate)\n     - Heating minimum (if applicable)\n  3. As CO2 rises above setpoint, zone minimum airflow increases\n  4. The AHU outdoor air fraction adjusts based on system-level\n     ventilation calculations (multiple-zone Vot equation from 62.1)\n```\n\n### CO2 Setpoint Selection\n\nThe CO2 setpoint depends on the outdoor CO2 level and the design ventilation rate:\n\n| Application | Typical CO2 Setpoint | Basis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Office (general) | 800-1000 ppm | 62.1 default occupant density and ventilation rates |\n| Conference room | 800-1000 ppm | Same as office, but with higher occupancy variation |\n| Classroom | 800-1000 ppm | Higher occupant density may require lower setpoint |\n| Gym/fitness | 1000-1200 ppm | Higher metabolic rates generate more CO2 per person |\n\nThese setpoints assume outdoor CO2 of approximately 400-420 ppm. If outdoor CO2 is higher, indoor setpoints should be adjusted accordingly.\n\n## Calibration and Maintenance\n\n| Task | Frequency | Method |\n|---|---|---|\n| Verify sensor reading against reference | Annually | Compare against a calibrated handheld CO2 meter |\n| Check ABC (automatic baseline correction) | Verify enabled at commissioning | Most NDIR sensors use ABC to correct drift; requires regular exposure to fresh air (~400 ppm) |\n| Inspect sensor for obstruction/damage | Annually | Visual inspection and cleaning |\n| Replace sensor | Per manufacturer recommendation (typically 10-15 years for NDIR) | Full replacement |\n| Verify outdoor air damper operation | Semi-annually | Command damper through full range; verify with airflow measurement |\n\n### ABC Considerations\n\nAutomatic Baseline Correction (ABC) assumes the sensor is periodically exposed to near-outdoor CO2 levels (approximately 400 ppm). This assumption fails in:\n\n- 24/7 occupied spaces (data centers, hospitals, some retail)\n- Spaces with poor night-flush ventilation\n- Spaces with CO2-generating processes\n\nIn these cases, disable ABC and calibrate manually per the manufacturer''s schedule.\n\n## Commissioning Checklist\n\n```\nSensor Verification:\n  [ ] Sensor reads within +/- 75 ppm of calibrated reference instrument\n  [ ] Sensor placement follows guidelines (breathing zone, away from\n      diffusers/doors)\n  [ ] Sensor output type matches BAS input configuration\n  [ ] Sensor wiring is correct and signal is stable\n\nControl Verification:\n  [ ] Outdoor air damper modulates in response to rising CO2\n  [ ] Minimum outdoor air position maintains area ventilation rate\n      (Ra component) when space is unoccupied\n  [ ] CO2 setpoint is documented and appropriate for the space type\n  [ ] Damper returns to minimum position when CO2 drops below setpoint\n  [ ] Economizer override works correctly (DCV does not restrict\n      outdoor air during economizer operation)\n\nDocumentation:\n  [ ] CO2 setpoint and basis documented\n  [ ] Sensor model, location, and calibration date recorded\n  [ ] Outdoor CO2 reference level documented\n  [ ] Minimum outdoor air position documented\n  [ ] Trend logging enabled for CO2, outdoor air damper, and\n      outdoor air flow (if measured)\n```\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Prevention |\n|---|---|\n| CO2 setpoint too high, under-ventilating | Calculate setpoint from 62.1 rates, do not use arbitrary values |\n| No minimum outdoor air position | Always maintain area ventilation rate regardless of CO2 |\n| Sensor near supply diffuser reads low | Relocate sensor to occupied zone away from direct supply air |\n| ABC fails in 24/7 spaces | Disable ABC and schedule manual calibration |\n| DCV overrides economizer | DCV minimum should not restrict outdoor air when economizer is active |\n| No outdoor CO2 reference | Establish outdoor CO2 baseline; do not assume 400 ppm everywhere |\n\n## References\n\n- [ASHRAE Standard 62.1 - Ventilation for Acceptable Indoor Air Quality](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/standards-62-1-62-2)\n- [ASHRAE Guideline 36 - High-Performance Sequences of Operation for HVAC Systems](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/guideline-36-high-performance-sequences-of-operation-for-hvac-systems)\n- [ASHRAE Handbook - HVAC Applications (Chapter on Ventilation and Infiltration)](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/ashrae-handbook)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

